WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.670
The Season 1 finale of "1883," the Paramount+
prequel series to Paramount's "Yellowstone,"

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.240
might just be the most heartbreaking episode
in the entire "Yellowstone" franchise.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.420
First and foremost, there are two major character
deaths in the finale, both of whom successfully

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.539
reach the end of their respective journeys
before passing.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:21.759
The first is Elsa Dutton, who, after being
gravely wounded by an arrow, makes her father,

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:24.970
James, promise that she can pick the spot
where she will die.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.920
She ends up choosing a small tree in the gorgeous
Paradise Valley and dies peacefully in her

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.169
father's arms.

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:35.090
The scene is gut-wrenching and tragic, though
the episode's second major death is by far

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.080
the more brutal of the two.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.950
In a time skip forward, we watch as Shea Brennan
finally completes his journey to the West

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.200
Coast, and sits on a beach in Oregon to watch
the waves crash against the shore.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.410
It was revealed earlier in the series that
Shea's only reason for making the journey

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.340
west was because of a promise he made to his
wife, Helen, who desperately wanted to see

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.340
the ocean.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.280
"Just look at that, Helen.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.190
Isn't it beautiful?"

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.320
Helen died prior to the series' start, so
Shea took it upon himself to let her see the

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.000
ocean through his eyes.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.210
Shea's promise is finally fulfilled, and after
staring at the waves for a while, he kills

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.590
himself so that he can finally be with his
wife once more.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:17.469
Given how well audiences had gotten to know
Shea throughout "1883" Season 1, his death

00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:18.920
is undeniably sad.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.960
Although, there is a silver lining that makes
the scene a bit less heartbreaking: the unexpected

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.950
appearance of a hummingbird on the beach,
which flits around for a moment looking right

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.539
at him, as if it's trying to get his attention.

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:33.459
It doesn't stay long, though, and when it
leaves, a smile widens across Shea's face,

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:36.679
who has finally found peace in his final moments.

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:40.770
The "1883" Reddit community naturally began
speculating on what this scene – and the

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.389
bird itself – means.

00:01:42.389 --> 00:01:46.170
A prevalent theory is that the hummingbird
symbolizes someone visiting Shea from beyond

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.179
the grave – specifically, his late wife
Helen.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:52.950
It could be that the hummingbird is indeed
Shea's wife, who comes to see him at the end

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.790
of their long journey to Oregon.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.810
After all, seeing what was supposed to be
their adventure through to the end was Shea's

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.899
driving purpose all along, so it makes thematic
sense.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:06.619
Still, other fans seem to believe that it's
actually an entirely different deceased character.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:07.619
One Reddit user wrote,

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:08.619
"It was Elsa.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:11.420
The symbolism of the hummingbird is the epitome
of her...

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.280
Remember her talking about birds in her final
moment?"

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.070
Other users referenced Elsa's conversation
with Shea earlier in the episode, which seems

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.110
to foreshadow his meeting with the bird.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.230
As one poster wrote,

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:25.020
"Elsa did tell him to save a spot for her
(at the beach) if she didn't survive."

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.030
It seems entirely possible that the hummingbird
is meant to symbolize Elsa coming back to

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.060
visit Shea just like she said she would.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.690
"Come meet ya on the beach."

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.880
"I'll save you a spot."

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.760
Then there's the third camp, which includes
people who just found the whole scene absolutely

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.890
tear-jerking, and didn't dwell much on the
symbolism at all.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.070
Calling the scene "sad but also beautiful,"
one person posted,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.280
"Shea on the beach with the hummingbird really
[got] me.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.450
[...] I know his body and soul were tired
and he was ready to be with his wife and daughter."

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.230
Other users wrote that they loved Shea and
Elsa's connection throughout the first season

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.900
of the series, all the way up to the bittersweet
end.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.730
Regardless of who the hummingbird really is,
its appearance is undeniably heartwarming

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.340
and makes an incredibly emotional scene even
more powerful.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.180
Shea Brennan's life was dominated by war,
loss, and sadness, which compounded to create

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.930
the gruff, no-nonsense attitude that became
his trademark.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.400
At least when he sat on that beach, looked
on at the vastness of the ocean, and had the

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.530
chance to breathe just a little easier, he
didn't have to do so alone.

